I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the frozen PXRD workflow first, then build a chemically screened phase inventory and refine it in GSAS-II. I’ll keep phase identification residual-led and verify that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and saved project all agree.

The chemical prior is CaCO₃ + WO₃ fired at 500 °C, so the initial inventory must include CaWO₄ plus plausible residual/decomposition phases—but I won’t assume that inventory from chemistry alone. I’m now extracting peak families and matching them against the frozen COD library, including alternate records/polymorphs where a plausible record fails.

The peak families already separate into three coherent groups: monoclinic WO₃ (the 23–25° triplet), scheelite CaWO₄, and calcite CaCO₃. The whole pattern is displaced by roughly +0.3° 2θ, so I’m comparing candidate records under one common angular correction before judging any record by scale or residual.

The three-phase inventory refines to nonzero scales and drops the baseline Rwp from ~30% to ~11%, which confirms all three phases are needed. The unconstrained common profile tried to use a negative W and an implausibly large U, so I’m rejecting that nuisance solution and returning to a nonnegative instrument baseline before adding phase-specific size/strain one phase at a time.

With size and strain separated, the common profile is now nonnegative (U = 0, W ≈ 13.5, X fixed at its lower trial bound) and the isolated families are broadly reproduced; Rwp is 8.78%. Calcite size is unsupported and ran to the no-broadening limit, so that term will be pruned. I’m comparing the alternate common zero correction and a second physically valid WO₃ record under this same nuisance model before refining intensities or anisotropy.

The displacement-only model is slightly better (Rwp 8.780% versus 8.819%) and requires much smaller phase-cell changes, so I’m retaining specimen displacement and fixing zero at zero. The fit has three strong, phase-specific scale contributions; I’m now re-running the physically valid triclinic WO₃ alternate under the identical profile restraints to make sure the selected monoclinic record is not just benefiting from nuisance flexibility.

Normalizing the COD records to the generator’s documented common isotropic ADP convention improved Rwp to 8.62% and stabilized the fractions near 57% WO₃, 12% CaWO₄, and 31% CaCO₃. I’m now profiling one shared Uiso per phase over the physically allowed range; this addresses the coherent high-angle intensity trend without adding atom-by-atom thermal parameters.

Common SH/L and polarization changes were unsupported, and the roughness pair was 100% correlated for only a 0.026-point Rwp gain, so I’m rejecting all three as nuisance surrogates. The next check is phase-width anisotropy: symmetry-distinct uniaxial directions will be challenged one phase at a time, with both strain components required to remain physical and replay-stable.

The phase inventory is stable at monoclinic WO₃, scheelite CaWO₄, and calcite CaCO₃. I’ve rejected alternate WO₃ records and unsupported common-profile terms; I’m now testing symmetry-distinct preferred-orientation axes before moving to restrained coordinate refinement and final replay verification.

WO₃ texture is unsupported: every tested axis stays essentially random and improves Rwp by at most 0.014 percentage point. CaWO₄ shows a possible [001] effect, so I’m completing all distinct axes and will retain it only if the improvement is reproducible and not acting as a scale/background surrogate.

The texture sweep found modest, phase-specific improvements: calcite [001] converged cleanly; the nominal CaWO₄ [111] solution did not (maximum shift/esd remained ~17), so it is provisional rather than accepted evidence. I’ll now refine the symmetry-allowed atomic coordinates from both textured and untextured baselines; this is the cleanest way to see whether those intensity trends are genuine orientation or merely compensating for source-structure mismatch.

The first coordinate test is decisive and physical in both branches: refining only the two WO₃ W sites lowers Rwp by about 0.24 point, converges to shift/esd <0.15, and moves the sites only 0.03–0.05 Å—exactly the scale expected from the synthetic source mismatch. Oxygen-site tests are now running.

The unconstrained O refinements are acting as surrogates: WO₃ O sites drift by 0.2–0.4 Å and CaWO₄ O by 0.14–0.18 Å, far beyond the generator’s 0.01–0.05 Å oxygen mismatch. I’m rejecting those coordinates. I’ll rebuild with only the physically supported WO₃ W displacement and the small calcite O shift, then repeat the texture challenge on that clean baseline.

After rebuilding the physical coordinate model, WO₃ again shows no credible texture (ratios remain within ~2% of random). The small calcite O shift is retained; I’m completing the CaWO₄/calcite axis replay to decide whether either orientation term survives without absorbing atomic-position error.

Both texture terms survive the clean-coordinate replay: CaWO₄ [111] refines to ~1.31 and calcite [001] to ~0.94, while calcite’s O shift contracts to a physically plausible ~0.013 Å. I’m retaining those terms provisionally, reconverging from disk, and then re-profiling the per-phase isotropic displacement values so the scales and fractions are not biased by the earlier coarse model.

The updated displacement profile has a clear WO₃ minimum near Uiso = 0.009 Å². CaWO₄ is trending higher than the earlier coarse estimate, so I’m letting that profile finish before fixing the phase scales; each trial is being fully re-refined rather than read from a single covariance step.

The profile widths and positions are now consistent across low, middle, and high angle, but several WO₃ intensities remain structured. Rather than release all 18 WO₃ oxygen coordinates—which produced nonphysical drift—I’m testing one independent O site at a time and will keep only replay-stable gains that preserve the known ≤0.05 Å aggregate mismatch envelope (with a small tolerance).

Only WO₃ O5 survives the site-wise challenge: it improves Rwp significantly for three coordinates while keeping the six-site oxygen RMS at 0.035 Å and the largest shift at 0.085 Å. The other O sites either offer negligible gain or require 0.14–0.27 Å displacements, so they remain at the published positions. I’m freezing the validated coordinates now and replaying all common scales, background, displacement, cells, and accepted profile terms from disk.

The final candidate is physically clean and stable: all three exported structures pass the contact/occupancy screen, no parameters are frozen, and save/reopen replay converges at max shift/esd 0.059. The crystalline fractions are 56.12% WO₃, 12.19% CaWO₄, and 31.69% CaCO₃; model variants move these by roughly 1–2 percentage points, larger than the covariance-only errors. I’m now producing the submission files and independently replaying `final.gpx` against the original pattern.

The independent replay succeeds and reproduces all three CIF exports exactly. One validation script assumed an older parser return shape; I’ve corrected the fit header to the scorer’s recognized format and am rerunning the submission parser and profile checks directly.

A final geometry audit found that continued joint refinement had let the WO₃ W-site RMS drift just beyond the generator’s 0.04 Å envelope, despite the earlier accepted W-only solution being within it. I’m correcting this with a one-dimensional profile along the refined displacement direction (0–0.04 Å), fixing the selected bounded value, and then regenerating/replaying the deliverables so the submitted structure remains physically defensible.

The bounded W-site model costs only 0.013 Rwp point and leaves fractions essentially unchanged, while satisfying the physical displacement envelope. The regenerated project now converges at shift/esd 0.011; I’m doing the final schema, geometry, hash, fraction, and profile-consistency checks on the exact files to be returned.

Completed and independently replay-verified.

Crystalline-normalized phase fractions:

- Monoclinic WO₃: 56.12 wt%
- Scheelite CaWO₄: 12.13 wt%
- Calcite CaCO₃: 31.75 wt%

Final fit: Rwp 7.7864%, Rp 5.9728%, GoF 3.2572. Estimated model-dependent fraction uncertainty is approximately ±2 wt% absolute.

